// SECURITY -- CAPABILITY ESCAPE via closure/fn-value laundering (FIXED --
// see `tools/loop/LEDGER.md`; the decisive proof is
// `cap_escape_closure_launder_deny.kry`).
//
// `zero_cap_tool` declares NO capabilities and legitimately should not have
// to -- it merely invokes a caller-supplied callback, exactly like
// `std::iter::map`/`filter`/`fold`. This file is the ORIGINAL finding that
// named the root cause: `fn_capabilities` (kryos-capabilities/src/
// checker.rs) was keyed purely by NAME, so calling a PARAMETER of function
// type (`reader()`) was invisible to it in every enforcement mode -- a
// closure built by privileged code and handed down as a callback silently
// carried its authority straight through a callee the checker believed held
// none.
//
// NOTE ON THIS SPECIFIC PROGRAM: `main` here is `@capabilities(fs:read)`
// regardless (it directly calls the ANNOTATED `make_secret_reader`, which
// already forces that declaration under the PRE-EXISTING, unrelated
// call-site propagation rule) -- so this exact program was never a genuine
// escalation past a boundary that lacked authority, and it still compiles
// clean post-fix, CORRECTLY: `main` legitimately holds `fs:read` and
// legitimately exercises it via the closure. The fix is proven by
// `cap_escape_closure_launder_deny.kry`, which shows the SAME laundering
// defeating a `deny!(fs:read)` narrowing -- a case where "the enclosing
// function already needs the capability anyway" does not apply. This file
// is kept as the documented origin of the finding and as a `kryos audit`
// blind-spot illustration (see docs/10-capabilities.md's "Known limitation"
// section): `kryos audit` is a pure syntactic scan of `@capabilities(...)`
// annotations and will never list `zero_cap_tool`, exactly like it never
// lists `map`/`filter` -- that is now understood to be CORRECT (a
// call-site-polymorphic function has no fixed capability to list), not a
// separate defect, once the enforcement fix below is in place.
//
// This is the closure/fn-value analogue of the raw-memory escape (str_to_ptr
// + ptr_byte_at) that was already closed -- it does not touch raw memory or
// FFI at all, so that fix did not cover it. It goes through the language's
// own capability-typed function values.

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn make_secret_reader(path: str) -> fn() -> str {
    return || file_read(path)
}

// Zero capabilities declared. Calling `reader()` -- a PARAMETER, not a named
// function/builtin -- is invisible to `fn_capabilities`, so this function's
// declared (strict mode) AND inferred (default mode) capability set is the
// EMPTY set, even though it performs a real `fs:read` at runtime.
fn zero_cap_tool(reader: fn() -> str) -> str {
    return reader()
}

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn main() {
    let reader = make_secret_reader("tests/security/secret_for_closure_launder.txt")
    let leaked = zero_cap_tool(reader)
    println("recovered via zero_cap_tool: " + leaked)
}
